# STATE.md - durable memory for THIS session

<!--
WHERE THIS FILE LIVES
  <project directory>/.agent-nexus/<session-name>-STATE.md

  One file PER SESSION, not per folder. Several sessions often work in the same
  project; a single shared STATE.md would have them overwriting each other's
  carry-forward without either one noticing. The name in the filename is the
  session name from sessions.md, so the owner of each notebook is obvious.

  It stays inside the project (rather than in the tool's state directory) because
  a managed session is denied write access to the control plane. Here, an
  ordinary file write is enough, and a human sees the notes next to the work.
  The tool creates .agent-nexus/ for you when a session is given a memory policy.

WHAT THIS IS
  A rolling notebook that survives a /clear, a /compact, a crash, or a context
  wipe. It is how a recurring or autonomous session carries facts from one run
  to the next.

HOW IT IS USED (managed-session `memory:` field)
  memory: read
    Every fresh or freshly-cleared brain is told to READ this file before acting.
    Writing is up to YOU: put your own "update STATE.md" instructions in the
    session's CLAUDE.md or the task's instruction file.
  memory: read-write
    Same read, plus every run automatically gets a protocol appended to its prompt
    telling it to WRITE this file back at the end (the sections below). It also
    flags you if this file grows past ~60 lines (see "on length").

THE WRITE PROTOCOL (what read-write injects each run)
  At the END of a run, update the sections below:
    - Last run:        replace with the current date + a 1-3 line summary.
    - Carry-forward:   add open / follow-up items; remove ones now done.
    - Issues encountered: log anything that went wrong or looked off.
    - For the human:   anything needing your attention, else "nothing".
  Never write secrets, tokens, or raw file contents here.

ON LENGTH (deliberately NOT auto-trimmed)
  We do not silently prune this file - useful history (especially "For the human"
  and "Issues encountered") would be lost. Instead: when a run reads this file and
  it is longer than ~60 lines, it flags that in the session, tells you the line
  count, and asks which sections can be cleared - and keeps asking every run until
  you resolve it. Clearing is a human decision.

Copy this file to <project>/.agent-nexus/<session-name>-STATE.md and delete these
comments (or keep them - they are harmless). Then set the session's `memory:`
field. A read-write session will create the file itself on its first run if you
would rather not seed it.
-->

## Last run
(none yet)

